The form disallows the ";" that we used to terminate the grep in the previous level.  So, in order to modify
our attack, we instead resort to a subshell using $().  Within the subshell, we can incrementally grep for
patterns against the /etc/natas_webpass/natas17 file.  If the internal grep succeeds, then you will get
no output in the outer grep on the dictionary (since the password string does not exist in the dictionary).
If the internal grep fails, then you will return a null from the subshell which will cause the outer grep
to return the entire dictionary.  So, the script does a binary search on the characters and numbers in
order to find the password.  Note, that you can use "^$(cut -c <col> /etc/natas_webpass/natas17)" to 
quickly identify characters in particular positions.

